<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Parley's configuration files: where they live, what they hold, and what they refuse.

    A project of its own because two programs read the manifest and they cannot reach a common
    copy any other way. Parley.Cli references the broker and through it the store; Parley.Mcp
    deliberately references neither, and the csproj next door says why — an adapter with no SQLite
    on its graph cannot open a database on a network share, which is the deployment that silently
    corrupts rather than failing. So the adapter cannot reference the CLI, and Parley.Core cannot
    hold the reader either, because Core has no I/O by design.

    The alternative was a second reader in the adapter, and the manifest exists precisely because
    two things resolving the same paths separately is how they come to disagree. Writing that
    failure into the fix would be a poor joke.

    Read by both; written only by Parley.Cli, which is where `project add` and `project remove`
    live. Nothing here knows what a broker is.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Parley.Configuration</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parley.Core\Parley.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
